  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 158 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nAnother weather system is expected to move in from the west late \nSaturday evening thanks to another wave traversing into the area \nunder the mid-level westerly regime. Surface cyclogenesis is \nexpected near the Oklahoma Panhandle resulting in increased \nsoutherly flow into the area that will lead to warm/moist advection. \nThis moisture combines with the mid-level forcing from the shortwave \nto produce shield of widespread rain across the area Saturday night \ninto Sunday morning. The REFS probabilities for >0.5\" of rain are \nnear 100% across the MO-IA border, where the greatest forcing is \nexpected with, 80% chances for that amount across northeast MO/west-\ncentral IL. With some low-level moisture return ahead of this \nsystem, the REFS also have mean SBCAPE values >50 J/kg across \nportions of southern MO/IL. While this is mostly insignificant, it \nis a signal that isolated rumbles of thunder and pockets of heavier \nshowers may be possible in southern portions of the area. Behind \nthis system, northerly low-level flow returns leading to CAA that \nknocks temperatures back to near seasonal normals on Monday. \nGuidance is in good agreement that w southerly flow returns on \nTuesday as a surface high slides to the east of the region leading \nto warming temperatures into the middle of the week. The LREF \nprobabilities for highs >50F (near seasonal normals) gets over 100% \nfor the whole area Wednesday into Thursday, highlighting the higher \nconfidence in above normal temperatures. By the end of the week, \nguidance reveals another trough sliding towards the region from the \nwest that would potentially bring another round of precipitation to \nthe region.",
